{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "description": "Colonies of an aerobic heterotroph and a lactic fermenter on a surface, under liquid that holds a quarter of air-saturated oxygen, as partly depleted saliva might. Oxygen and glucose diffuse down from the liquid; inside the larger colonies oxygen runs out, the fermenter turns glucose into lactate, and the lactate diffuses out to heterotrophs nearer the oxygen. The chemistry is that of glucose_cross_feeding.json. Rates, yields, half-saturation constants and the biomass density (2000 C-mol per m3) are illustrative (confidence C). Diffusivities are effective values inside a biofilm (docs/parameters.md), used everywhere until diffusivity depends on the biofilm. Biomass grows in place: colonies do not yet spread, so the run is kept short.",
